peranto but not in  any meaningful sense or meaningful  quantity you're unlikely to go to  esperanto land and sit beside  even in an airplane sit beside someone  who can speak esperanto so i'm not  motivated so if we get back to  interslavic  i guess it's possible to come up with  some kind of a sort of neutral slavic  language which reduces the sort of  idiosyncrasies of each slavic language  which is easily written in cyrillic and  latin you know alphabet  but i would i learned that language i  started out with russian because it has  the largest numbers of speaker speakers  it has the best known  literature culture and so forth it's a  huge country it's tremendously  attractive to learn russian to be to be  introduced to that world would i take  the interim step of learning  into slavic i don't think so  uh  i i having learned now a number of  slavic languages to varying degrees of  proficiency i think in a way  slovak kind of does that because slovak  is seems to be the most easily  understood slow slow back or  slavic language kind of closer to polish  closer to ukrainian even closer to some  of the south slavic languages  and equally distant from russian as is  czech or polish so  um  you know and there you have a country  you have a history and you can go and  visit bratislava and eat their food and  stuff so i'm not a fan of that the same  with the romance languages interlingua  why wouldn't you just learn spanish  which is the most widely spoken of the  romance languages and then use that as a  base to then focus on other  romance languages which will then be  easier because you have actually learned  one of them  uh  so there you have it interslavic  interlingua that's kind of my feeling  same with esperanto i understand that  esperanto is easy to learn and i have  i understand that it would be a useful  subject to have in school young children  who essentially will learn whatever you  ask them to learn  and if they learn esperanto and they  have immediate success in being able to  use the language that may very well  encourage them to learn other languages  so i see that as quite useful but as an  adult i don't i wouldn't you know i need  to get  sort of really into the language  which is what i'm doing with arabic you  know i am sure that it would be far  easier for me to learn esperanto uh it's  been about a month since i started into  arabic my ipad is never far away i try  to get in an hour an hour and a half of  of linking and listening and i still  don't understand very well when i listen  but i do have a sense that i am  improving  uh you know you can study the arabic  letters and then when you see them again  in the word and you keep making the same  mistakes and so  at times it's frustrating but but i i  had the sense this morning that i was  actually getting a little bit better  which reminds me of something else that  i'll finish off on and that is  you know  people often sort of say well are you a  linguist or are you a polyglot well i  really wasn't familiar that familiar  with the word polyglot 10 years ago and  i asked around in our office and most  this is in vancouver and i would say  that at least 10 years ago most native  speakers of english didn't know what the  word polyglot meant  most native speakers of english  understood the word linguist to mean  someone who speaks many languages or is  good at languages at languages he's  quite the linguist he speaks many  languages  now i've become more familiar with the  word polyglot so i tend to use the word  polyglot more  and linguist is used for people who  study linguistics now in the linguistics  area there are a number of terms  that are used in applied linguistics uh  one of which is interlanguage  and interlanguage is sort of this  concept that when we are learning a  second language we tend to use a  language that is a mixture of our own  and the  language we're trying to learn  i've never understand why i understood  why that would be a useful  term because  you know i'm nowhere in arabic  and there will be a gradual continuum  until i get good at arabic  those languages which are similar to our  own i think those are the languages  where  often  we will maintain  some phrases from our  own language  for example i hear swedish people and i  have done a lot of business in sweden  who are very fluent in english  uh oh and by the way i was very  impressed in listening to the norwegian  uh prime minister speaking  so well in english and a lot of the  scandinavians are very fluent in english  but there are certain phrases that that  remain there from their native language  for example in english we say there are  there is  there are  lots of trees in  vancouver  and lots of swedish people will say it  is many trees because that's how it's  said in swedish or german people who say  i have lived in canada since many years  instead of four many years however while  they speak there are these remnants  you know it can also go the other way  where you know and i should say that if  the languages are more different then  there are phrases that you learn  in that  new language which are so different from  your own that there's no chance  uh  you know of confusing you you start  saying things automatically like um  like how about that there is no  equivalent in english so you just  naturally start to say these phrases in  the language  you know i saw think we don't say i so  think in english but we said yeah i  think so so  as we you know get better at the  language we start to  sort of start using those natural  phrases of the language without thinking  of our own but of course there will be  some vestiges of  might be your own language might be  another language so is inter language  really something useful or is it just a  matter of of we are gradually  progressing from a very limited  knowledge in the language to where we  have more and more of the natural  phrases of that language  uh but at times  and particularly for people who speak  many languages they you know we could in  fact be influenced by some other  language so there is always  interference so yeah to that extent  interlanguage exists but i'm not sure  that it describes anything  that has any particular application to  anything we just have to learn the  language hopefully we're motivated to  learn the language and we just continue  as i'm doing with arabic and we struggle  and we have  you know the victories seem few and far  between but lo and behold  even though we felt all the time that we  weren't making much progress after six  months a year or so we find that we're  able to handle the language even with  mistakes whether these mistakes are  influenced by our own language or  another language or just the fact that  we can't remember how to say things  properly anyway bit of a ramble  uh i hope that answers the gentleman who  asked about andrew slavic and uh bye for  now  [Music]  you
 

